      "content": "# Critical Review: Quantum Coherence in Room-Temperature Topological Insulators\n\n## Methodological Concerns Regarding Sample Quality\nThe reported 300K coherence observations in the bismuth-based topological insulator are heavily compromised by the explicitly stated low sample purity. In topological insulators, defects introduce localized states that act as decoherence channels, yet the manuscript fails to provide a definitive carrier concentration measurement or susceptibility data to rule out these parasitic mechanisms. Without quantifying the non-topological contributions, the attribution of the observed signals to genuine long-lived coherence remains speculative.\n\n## Statistical Rigor and Error Analysis\nThe error bars on the coherence time are unusually large for spectroscopic data intended to establish a fundamental physical limit, suggesting insufficient averaging or unaccounted photon statistics. A robust claim requiring nature-level confidence should include time-resolved fits with significance tests exceeding the standard of p < 0.01; the current analysis appears descriptive rather than statistical, leaving the reliability of the extracted decay constants in doubt.\n\n## Disconnect Between Claims and Evidence\nThe conclusion of robust room-temperature coherence does not logically follow from the noisy, low-fidelity datasets presented. The authors gloss over the exponential decay trends where the signal-to-noise",
